<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>{title}</title><meta name="description" content="Throw a beginner-friendly Mahjong night with welcoming invitations, a comfortable table setup, snacks and simple tips."/><link rel="canonical" href="https://mymahjongera.com/lifestyle/mahjong-night/"/><style>body{font:18px/1.75 Arial,sans-serif;margin:0;background:#fffaf7;color:#4d2540}main{max-width:770px;margin:60px auto;padding:0 24px}h1{font: bold clamp(38px,6vw,60px)/1.1 Georgia,serif}h2{font-family:Georgia,serif;margin-top:44px}a{color:#ad4270}nav{padding:22px;background:#fce8ee}</style></head><body><nav><a href="/">← My Mahjong Era</a></nav><main><p>LIFESTYLE · GET TOGETHER</p><h1>A Mahjong night everyone wants to come back to</h1><p>A beautiful game night starts with an invitation, not an expensive tile set. Gather four people, put the kettle on, and make space for learning.</p><h2>Set the table</h2><p>Choose a level table with room for four racks, tiles and drinks kept safely away from play. Give everyone a clearly lit seat.</p><h2>Choose one ruleset</h2><p>American, Chinese regional and Japanese riichi Mahjong are not interchangeable. Pick the variation before inviting beginners and prepare the correct reference material.</p><h2>Make first-timers comfortable</h2><p>Share a two-minute explanation of the goal, agree to pause for questions, and let everyone keep a quick reference sheet nearby.</p><h2>Bring snacks and a little personality</h2><p>Easy finger food, tea, cheerful napkins and a playlist can make the gathering feel special. Keep the tiles and drinks apart.</p><h2>Keep score simply</h2><p>Our <a href="/#tools">free score tracker</a> lets you record agreed points for four people. It deliberately does not judge official hands.</p><p><a href="/">Back to My Mahjong Era</a></p></main></body></html>